.page sort 1 sort lines of text

# SYNOPSIS
*sort* [*-rnfubsc*] [*-k* _KEY_] [*-t* _SEP_] [*-o* _FILE_] [_FILE_...]

# DESCRIPTION
`sort` reads all the lines of its input (standard input if no file is named), sorts them and writes the result. The sort is stable when `-s` or `-u` is given; otherwise lines that compare equal on the key are put in order by comparing the whole lines, so the output does not depend on the input order.

# OPTIONS
@ *-r*
    Reverse the result.
@ *-n*
    Compare keys as numbers. Leading blanks are skipped and the text is read as a decimal number, so 10 comes after 9.
@ *-f*
    Ignore case when comparing.
@ *-u*
    Write only the first of each run of lines whose keys are equal.
@ *-b*
    Ignore leading blanks in the key.
@ *-s*
    Stable: lines with equal keys keep their input order.
@ *-c*
    Do not sort; check whether the input is already sorted. If not, print `disorder at line N` and exit 1.
@ *-k* _START_[,_END_]
    Sort on the fields from _START_ to _END_ (counted from 1) instead of the whole line. A field ends at white space, or at _SEP_ when `-t` is used. After a field number the letters `n`, `f`, `r` and `b` apply that option to this key alone; a `.`_CHAR_ offset is accepted and ignored.
@ *-t* _SEP_
    Use the single character _SEP_ to separate fields.
@ *-o* _FILE_
    Write the result to _FILE_ instead of standard output.

The options `-g` and `-h` are treated like `-n`; `-M`, `-d`, `-i` and `-z` are accepted and ignored.

# EXAMPLES
    sort names.txt
    sort -rn scores.txt | head -3
    sort -t: -k3 -n /Hakurei/etc/passwd
    sort -u list.txt
    ls -l /bin | sort -k5 -n

# EXIT STATUS
0; with `-c` 1 when the input is out of order; 2 for a bad option.

# SEE ALSO
uniq(1), comm(1), cut(1)
